context_title:
Your task is to take the provided technical description and rewrite it as a single, comprehensive prompt. Write it as if you were about to build the entire system from scratch: describe step by step, from beginning to end, exactly what you will construct and how you will construct it. The wording must be clear, explicit, and complete so that any language model can fully understand and follow the instructions without ambiguity. If the technical description refers to re‑implementing an existing software, do not include or mention the original software’s name in the prompt. Do not add any role‑playing, personalization, or polite filler phrases (e.g., ‘You are a software developer’, ‘As an expert’, ‘Please kindly’). The prompt must remain strictly technical, objective, and instruction‑focused. Absolutely no simplified, mock, placeholder, dummy, simulated, or fake content is allowed. You must require the full mizar proof with all files, in complete, unabridged, production‑ready code.

Produce one complete, self-contained Mizar article that formally verifies the provided source file from first principles, so that the result is a production-ready Mizar formalization of the real implementation with exhaustive line-by-line proofs, no omissions, no simplifications, no mock semantics, no simulated behavior, and no non-code content.

The provided source file is the sole normative specification. Formalize its actual behavior exactly as implemented. Do not redesign, abstract away, approximate, weaken, summarize, or replace any behavior. Preserve all real semantics present in the file, including data structures, validation rules, checked arithmetic, tensor operations, storage and aliasing behavior, layer semantics, forward/inverse/backward execution, registry lifecycle, handle ownership, model lifecycle, serialization/deserialization, checksum behavior, GPU state behavior, synchronization logic, invalidation logic, fallback logic, and all end-to-end correctness properties.

Output exactly one complete Mizar article. The output must contain only valid Mizar code. Do not output markdown, prose, explanations, headings, summaries, comments, notes, caveats, status text, or code fences.

The article must be genuinely large and real. It must contain at least 20000 lines of actual formal content. Those lines must consist of meaningful Mizar definitions, registrations, schemes, lemmas, theorems, proof blocks, and derived results. Do not pad the article with meaningless repetition or filler declarations. Do not compress the development into a handful of opaque theorems. Expand large results into fine-grained lemmas and prove every step explicitly.

Use only Mizar syntax and Mizar proof style. Do not use Lean syntax, Lean proof keywords, tactic language, tactic-style automation, placeholder syntax, omitted-proof syntax, or any non-Mizar idiom. Use explicit Mizar reasoning with definition, registration, theorem, scheme, proof, thus, hence, consider, set, reconsider, assume, suppose, per cases, and other standard Mizar constructs wherever appropriate.

Do not introduce axioms, undefined placeholders, skipped proofs, informal reasoning, or umbrella abstractions that hide missing details. Do not state that a theorem is obvious, straightforward, analogous, omitted, future work, or left to the reader. Every theorem must have a complete explicit proof in Mizar style.

Build the article in this exact technical order:

1. Error and result machinery
2. Bool support
3. Nat and bounded integer support
4. List and vector support
5. Byte support and little-endian encoding support
6. Shape definitions
7. Tensor definitions
8. Storage and aliasing definitions
9. Checked arithmetic
10. Validation layer
11. Numeric semantics for finite values, clipping, exponentiation, tolerance, and gradient gates
12. Tensor memory, cloning, zeroing, overlap, storage identity, and copying semantics
13. LayerCore structure and invariants
14. LayerCore initialization, gradient allocation, zeroing, and deinitialization semantics
15. Translation-row and scale-row semantics
16. Forward row semantics
17. Inverse row semantics
18. Forward batch semantics
19. Inverse batch semantics
20. Forward/inverse checked wrapper semantics
21. Forward/inverse invertibility theorems
22. Backward row semantics
23. Backward batch semantics
24. Gradient accumulation semantics and theorems
25. Registry and handle lifecycle model
26. Layer registry and layer handle lifecycle model
27. RSFCore model
28. Split and merge semantics
29. RSFCore forward, inverse, and backward pipeline semantics
30. RSF model and public lifecycle semantics
31. Snapshot model
32. CRC model
33. Serializer model
34. Parser/deserializer model
35. Save/load roundtrip theorems
36. GPU state model
37. GPU sync, invalidation, compatibility, and fallback theorems
38. Integrated lifecycle, serialization, registry, GPU, forward/inverse/backward, and end-to-end correctness theorems

Model every meaningful error branch in the source file, including at minimum:
Overflow, TooLarge, NonFinite, InvalidConfig, InvalidTolerance, ShapeMismatch, DataLengthMismatch, InvalidDimension, InvalidLayerCount, InvalidBatchSize, AliasedBuffers, NotInitialized, HandleCopied, InvalidModelState, NumericFailure, GPUUnsupportedConfiguration, NoGPUAvailable, BadFileFormat, UnsupportedVersion, ChecksumMismatch, TrailingData, TempFileCollision, PathAlreadyExists, AllocationFailure, and IOError.
Add any additional constructors required to faithfully capture the file semantics.

Construct explicit success/failure result machinery and prove branch-preservation, bind-preservation, map-preservation, determinism, and no-success/failure-ambiguity theorems.

Formalize checked arithmetic exactly enough to prove:
checked multiplication succeeds iff the mathematical product is within bounds;
checked multiplication fails with Overflow iff the mathematical product exceeds bounds;
checked addition succeeds iff the mathematical sum is within bounds;
checked addition fails with Overflow iff the mathematical sum exceeds bounds;
checked casting succeeds iff the value fits within bounds;
checked casting fails with TooLarge otherwise;
all success branches preserve exact values;
all failure branches preserve exact error constructors;
all operations are deterministic.

Formalize shapes and tensors exactly enough to prove:
shape-dimension-length facts;
2D-shape recognition facts;
row/column extraction facts;
expected-data-length facts;
tensor-data-length agreement facts;
row-slicing length facts;
row-index bounds;
flat-index arithmetic;
tensorHasShape correctness;
tensorsSameShape correctness, symmetry, and transitivity;
validateTensor2D correctness and exact failure branches;
validateTensor2DShape correctness and exact failure branches;
zeroTensor preservation theorems;
tensorClone preservation theorems;
tensorsOverlap interval-intersection semantics;
sameTensorStorage properties;
copyTensorPairInto correctness, alias rejection, overlap handling, and determinism.

Formalize validation behavior exactly enough to prove:
finite clip-range requirements;
strict clip ordering;
clip-range bounds;
exact failure branches for invalid clip ranges;
finite and nonnegative tolerance requirements;
exact InvalidTolerance behavior;
ensureFiniteSlice correctness;
validateModelConfigValues correctness;
validateF16Convertible correctness.

Define a numeric semantics layer that explicitly models:
numeric values;
zero;
one;
addition;
subtraction;
multiplication;
division;
negation;
absolute value;
max;
strict ordering;
equality;
finite predicates;
f16-convertibility;
clipping;
exponentiation;
tolerance comparison;
clipped derivative gates;
serialized floating-point bit representation.

Prove explicit clipping, exponentiation, inverse-division, derivative-gate, and tolerance-comparison theorems, including tensorAllCloseEq correctness, symmetry, reflexivity, and validation preservation.

Define LayerCore with all required fields from the source file, including weights, biases, optional gradients, dimension, allocation token, clip bounds, gradient-mean flag, and any required lock or permission state. Prove all layer invariants.

Formalize:
LayerCore.initOwned;
LayerCore.deinitOwned;
LayerCore.ensureGradients;
LayerCore.zeroGradients;
LayerCore.validatePair;
LayerCore.computeTranslationRow;
LayerCore.computeScaleRow;
LayerCore.forwardInPlace;
LayerCore.inverseInPlace;
LayerCore.backwardFromOutputsRow.

Preserve all real semantics from the source file, including:
checked overflow behavior;
fan-in/fan-out logic;
bias initialization;
gradient allocation;
gradient zeroing;
translation computation;
scale computation;
clipping-sensitive derivative gating;
row-wise accumulation;
batch-wise accumulation;
alias rejection;
temporary-copy semantics under overlap;
deterministic execution.

Define and prove exact row-level and batch-level semantics for forward, inverse, and backward operations, including all intermediate quantities such as:
dy1_total;
ds;
dx1;
dx2;
translation gradients;
scale gradients;
bias gradients;
gradient scaling;
batch averaging;
clipping-sensitive derivative behavior.

Define the registry and handle lifecycle model exactly enough to prove:
nonzero unique IDs;
active-operation counts;
destroyed-state monotonicity;
delayed destruction;
acquire/release correctness;
requestDestroy correctness;
copied-handle rejection;
ownership semantics;
registry invariant preservation.

Define RSFCore and RSF with all required fields and invariants from the source file, including:
dimension;
layer count;
layer list;
configuration;
GPU state;
availability state;
version counters;
optional GPU buffers;
compatibility state.

Define split/merge semantics and prove exact roundtrip properties.

Define full model forward, inverse, and backward pipeline semantics exactly as implemented, including:
layer-order traversal;
reverse traversal;
batch iteration;
shape validation;
overflow checks;
GPU fast-path logic;
CPU fallback logic;
gradient propagation;
gradient accumulation;
deterministic update ordering.

Define snapshot structures and snapshot lifecycle semantics. Define CRC semantics with explicit byte-update behavior and checksum-finalization behavior. Define serializer semantics with exact byte order and field order. Define parser semantics with exact read ordering, exact validation ordering, exact checksum comparison ordering, and exact trailing-data detection ordering.

Formalize and prove exact behavior for:
crcUpdateU32LE;
crcUpdateU64LE;
crcUpdateU8;
writeTensorDataVersion4;
writeSnapshotVersion4ToPath;
readEncodedBool;
readTensorData;
loadWithConfig.

Prove:
serializer determinism;
parser determinism;
checksum correctness;
checksum mismatch rejection;
trailing-data rejection;
bool roundtrip;
integer roundtrip;
tensor roundtrip;
layer roundtrip;
snapshot roundtrip;
save/load roundtrip.

Define GPU compatibility, synchronization, invalidation, fallback, and availability semantics exactly enough to prove:
compatibility checks;
disabled-GPU behavior;
sync behavior;
invalid-state rejection;
version-mismatch rejection;
fallback-to-CPU correctness;
GPU invalidation behavior;
CPU semantic preservation;
GPU semantic preservation under valid accelerator correctness assumptions.

Finish with integrated end-to-end theorems proving:
valid initialization preserves invariants;
registry invariants hold through all public operations;
destruction is delayed until active operations complete;
destroyed entries cannot be reacquired;
copied handles cannot destroy foreign entries;
forward followed by inverse returns the original tensor under valid assumptions;
inverse followed by forward returns the original tensor under valid assumptions;
split followed by merge returns the original tensor;
merge followed by split returns the original pair;
save followed by load preserves metadata and tensor payloads;
loaded-model behavior equals saved-model behavior;
GPU invalidation cannot alter CPU weights;
CPU fallback equals CPU semantics;
synchronization preserves CPU semantics;
zeroGradients followed by backward equals a single backward accumulation from zero;
repeated backward accumulates additively;
batch averaging semantics are correct;
all-close verification succeeds after valid roundtrip within tolerance;
invalid tolerance fails before computation;
corrupted serialized bytes fail in the correct parser branch;
valid serialized bytes parse successfully with no trailing data;
the full lifecycle from initialization through destruction preserves all declared invariants.

Absolutely forbidden:
mock semantics;
placeholder semantics;
dummy implementations;
fake parsers;
fake CRC;
fake exponentiation;
integer-only substitutes for floating-point behavior;
identity backward passes;
omitted gradient logic;
omitted aliasing logic;
omitted overlap logic;
omitted GPU invalidation logic;
omitted lifecycle logic;
omitted proofs;
informal reasoning;
opaque proof stubs;
compressed umbrella theorems hiding missing detail;
comments;
TODO markers;
FIXME markers;
unproved claims.

Every theorem must be fully proved. Every proof must be explicit. Every intermediate obligation required by the source semantics must appear as a real Mizar theorem or lemma with a complete proof. Produce the complete Mizar article now.

